PUBLIC RESPONSE: The key complication here is effect heterogeneity (Y(1) and Y(0) differ across units). In that case any two valid instruments generally estimate different weighted averages of heterogeneous LATEs, so a conventional over-identification statistic such as the Hansen J-test has a non-zero population value even when every instrument satisfies exclusion and exogeneity. The Anderson-Rubin statistic is likewise a joint test on the reduced form and inherits the same problem; a t-test has no over-identification content at all. Consequently none of the listed procedures satisfies the logical requirement that population rejection implies at least one instrument violates exclusion/exogeneity. The correct choice is therefore E.

(The reasoning above is independent of any finite-sample or weak-instrument refinements; it follows directly from the population moment conditions under heterogeneity.)

}

